	<key>readme</key>
	<string>A Raycast-style window for Alfred's clipboard history: the list on the left, a preview and an Information panel on the right. Alfred keeps recording the clipboard; this only reads its history.

- ⌃⌘V opens and closes it (the keyword clipboard and Home open it too)
- Configure Workflow… at the top of the workflow in Alfred Preferences sets another hotkey, or a bar of cards along the bottom of the screen like Paste's
- Type to filter; the menu at the top right picks Text, Links, Code, Media or Colors
- ↑↓ move, ↩ or double-click pastes into the app you were in, ⌘↩ copies, Esc closes
- ⌘-click or ⇧-click selects several to paste, copy or drag at once; texts paste as one, a line each
- Drag a row out: text drags as text, images and files as files; shake for a Dropover shelf
- Drag the window by its background to move it; it remembers where

The app (ClipHistory.app, built from source/ClipboardHistory.swift) starts at login through the LaunchAgent com.andreisuslov.alfred-clipboard-history and registers the hotkey itself, which is what lets it take the keyboard the way Alfred does. It has no Accessibility permission of its own: to paste, it runs the "paste" trigger and Alfred presses ⌘V for it.</string>
